s."}, {"name": "Technical & Cross-functional Leadership", "weight": 35, "description": "Explains engineering decisions, collaboration with stakeholders, and technical leadership."}, {"name": "System Scale & Complexity", "weight": 30, "description": "Details scale, throughput, reliability requirements, or AI integration scope of systems owned."}], "critical_failures": ["Vague description of personal role without specific technical contributions or ownership.", "Unable to explain architectural decisions or system scope in previous work."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-007"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'Can you describe your experience working as an agentic engineer, including building and deploying agent-based systems?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Engineering Leadership & Experience", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "CAREER_PROGRESSION_OWNERSHIP", "reference_answer": "Candidate articulates hands-on engineering experience designing, implementing, and deploying agentic AI systems. Discusses personal technical ownership across prompt engineering, tool integration, orchestration frameworks (e.g., LangGraph), state persistence, and evaluation pipelines. Shares realistic production challenges: debugging non-deterministic failures, mitigating tool calling errors, managing context windows, and iterating from POC prototypes to production-grade deployments with monitoring and guardrails.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Role Scope & Ownership", "weight": 35, "description": "Describes clear responsibilities, architecture ownership, and system impact in recent roles."}, {"name": "Technical & Cross-functional Leadership", "weight": 35, "description": "Explains engineering decisions, collaboration with stakeholders, and technical leadership."}, {"name": "System Scale & Complexity", "weight": 30, "description": "Details scale, throughput, reliability requirements, or AI integration scope of systems owned."}], "critical_failures": ["Vague description of personal role without specific technical contributions or ownership.", "Unable to explain architectural decisions or system scope in previous work."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-008"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'Can you discuss your experience with MCP and why it has not yet been used in production systems?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Model Context Protocol & Tools", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "TOOL_INTEGRATION_MCP", "reference_answer": "Model Context Protocol (MCP) standardizes how models discover, authorize, and invoke external resources and tools via JSON-RPC 2.0 (over stdio or SSE). It decouples client agent reasoning from server-side tool execution, providing standardized schemas, access control, and isolation. Trade-offs in production include RPC latency, authorization overhead, and debugging complexity compared to native direct function calls.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "MCP Architecture & Transport: Explains client-server decoupling, JSON-RPC 2.0, and dynamic tool discovery", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Decouples model host/client from tool server", "Uses JSON-RPC 2.0 via stdio or SSE/HTTP", "Dynamic schema discovery without hardcoding"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Security, Authorization & Schemas: Details typed JSON schemas, role-based authorization, and isolated tool execution", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Typed JSON schema validation for parameters", "Role-based authorization and tool permissions (RBAC)", "Execution isolation and sandboxing"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Production Trade-offs & Adoption: Evaluates latency overhead, enterprise readiness, and comparison with direct function calls", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["RPC overhead and network latency", "Standardization across multiple agents vs custom code", "Observability, distributed tracing, and error handling"]}], "critical_failures": ["Confuses MCP with a proprietary model architecture rather than a protocol", "Claims MCP eliminates the need for parameter schema validation"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-009"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'Can you explain a real-world scenario where you used multi-agent systems and MCP for tool communication and task execution?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Model Context Protocol & Tools", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "TOOL_INTEGRATION_MCP", "reference_answer": "Model Context Protocol (MCP) standardizes how models discover, authorize, and invoke external resources and tools via JSON-RPC 2.0 (over stdio or SSE). It decouples client agent reasoning from server-side tool execution, providing standardized schemas, access control, and isolation. Trade-offs in production include RPC latency, authorization overhead, and debugging complexity compared to native direct function calls.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "MCP Architecture & Transport: Explains client-server decoupling, JSON-RPC 2.0, and dynamic tool discovery", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Decouples model host/client from tool server", "Uses JSON-RPC 2.0 via stdio or SSE/HTTP", "Dynamic schema discovery without hardcoding"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Security, Authorization & Schemas: Details typed JSON schemas, role-based authorization, and isolated tool execution", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Typed JSON schema validation for parameters", "Role-based authorization and tool permissions (RBAC)", "Execution isolation and sandboxing"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Production Trade-offs & Adoption: Evaluates latency overhead, enterprise readiness, and comparison with direct function calls", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["RPC overhead and network latency", "Standardization across multiple agents vs custom code", "Observability, distributed tracing, and error handling"]}], "critical_failures": ["Confuses MCP with a proprietary model architecture rather than a protocol", "Claims MCP eliminates the need for parameter schema validation"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-010"}', 1),
