tails typed JSON schemas, role-based authorization, and isolated tool execution", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Typed JSON schema validation for parameters", "Role-based authorization and tool permissions (RBAC)", "Execution isolation and sandboxing"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Production Trade-offs & Adoption: Evaluates latency overhead, enterprise readiness, and comparison with direct function calls", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["RPC overhead and network latency", "Standardization across multiple agents vs custom code", "Observability, distributed tracing, and error handling"]}], "critical_failures": ["Confuses MCP with a proprietary model architecture rather than a protocol", "Claims MCP eliminates the need for parameter schema validation"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-012"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'Can you explain your understanding of the company''s product and how agentic AI is applied to automate compliance workflows in financial organizations?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "System Architecture & Design", "subcategory": "Domain Workflow Automation", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "Domain-specific agentic automation decomposes unstructured, multi-step business workflows into specialized agent roles with domain tools. In recruiting or compliance workflows, dedicated agents handle parsing, criteria evaluation, bias auditing, and decision support with audit logging. To maintain enterprise trust and compliance, autonomous agents provide recommendations with structured rationale and citations, while irreversible decisions require human review.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Agent Roles & Coordination", "weight": 40, "description": "Explains agent roles, supervisor/orchestrator responsibilities, and routing mechanisms."}, {"name": "State & Communication", "weight": 30, "description": "Details shared state management, tool usage, and inter-agent message passing."}, {"name": "Validation & Fallback", "weight": 30, "description": "Covers tool validation, retry mechanisms, and human-in-the-loop escalation."}], "critical_failures": ["Describes a single monolithic LLM prompt without agent decomposition or state routing.", "Fails to specify how subagents coordinate or pass execution state."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-013"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Python & Data Manipulation', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'Can you talk to me more about how you use agent coding tools and how that helps you as an engineer do your job?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Python & Data Manipulation", "subcategory": "AI-Assisted Software Engineering", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "CODING_DEVELOPER_PRODUCTIVITY", "reference_answer": "AI coding assistants and companion agents (e.g. Cursor, Copilot, automated PR review agents) accelerate development through context-aware autocompletion, boilerplate generation, AST-aware code navigation, and test generation. To prevent regressions, outputs must undergo strict deterministic verification: syntax linting, static analysis, unit test execution, and human code review. Measurable impact is tracked through reduced PR review turnaround time, test coverage improvements, and developer velocity while mitigating risks around hallucinated APIs and security vulnerabilities.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Workflow Integration & Tooling: Explains how coding agents integrate into development environments (IDEs, CI/CD PR bots, AST tools)", "weight": 40, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Integration with IDEs or GitHub PR workflows", "Automating boilerplate, docstrings, or unit tests", "Repository indexing and AST context retrieval"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Verification & Quality Assurance: Details verification safeguards before committing AI-generated code", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Mandatory unit test execution and static analysis", "Human-in-the-loop code review approval", "Catching security vulnerabilities or hallucinated APIs"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Productivity Metrics & Operational Limitations: Discusses measurable productivity gains and awareness of tool limitations", "weight": 25, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Tracking metrics like PR turnaround time or test coverage", "Handling context window limits in large mono-repos", "Addressing licensing, privacy, and code IP concerns"]}], "critical_failures": ["Advocates blindly merging AI-generated code without manual review or automated testing", "Cannot explain how coding agents assist software engineering beyond basic string autocompletion"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-014"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'Could you just explain in short your flow like how does it work and how many agents did you build apart from your supervisor agent and how many tools you have like integrated in each of the agents?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Multi-Agent System Architecture", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "The system employs a hierarchical supervisor-worker multi-agent architecture to manage complex domain tasks. An Orchestrator/Supervisor agent maintains global workflow state, performs intent classification, and delegates domain sub-tasks to specialized domain agents (e.g., Knowledge Agent, Transaction Agent, Policy Agent). Agents communicate via structured schemas over a shared state graph. Tools are decoupled from reasoning, exposed as typed functions with strict parameter validation. Resilience is ensured through self-reflection loops for transient errors, recursion depth limits to prevent infinite execution cycles, and escalation paths to human operators.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Multi-Agent Roles & Architecture: Defines clear agent roles, responsibilities, and separation of concerns across domain boundaries", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Supervisor / Orchestrator coordination pattern", "Specialized domain workers with dedicated tools", "Decoupling reasoning from execution"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Coordination, State & Communication: Explains how state and message payloads flow between supervisor and domain agents", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Shared state dictionary and context passing", "Structured input/output contracts between agents", "Parallel vs sequential sub-task execution"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Validation, Fallback & Escalation: Covers error handling, infinite loop prevention, guardrail validation, and human handoff", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Step/depth limits (max iterations)", "Output validation and reflection loops", "Graceful fallback and human escalation"]}], "critical_failures": ["Designs a monolithic prompt instead of specialized agent roles", "Lacks mechanisms to prevent infinite agent execution loops"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-015"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'Describe your intent detection strategy, including the roles of the router agent, orchestrator agent, and LLM for reasoning within the agentic workflow.', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Routing & Planning", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ROUTING_AND_PLANNING", "reference_answer": "Routing and planning govern how queries are classified, decomposed, and executed. The router performs intent classification using semantic embeddings, few-shot classifiers, or fast small LLMs with structured output schemas. Complex queries are decomposed by a planner agent into directed sub-tasks with dependency graphs. Ambiguous intents trigger interactive clarification turns rather than hallucinations, while unrecognized queries route to fallback domain agents or human escalation queues.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Intent Classification & Task Decomposition: Explains intent detection strategies and decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Semantic embedding search or few-shot LLM classification", "Structured JSON output schema for routing tags", "Decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks with dependencies"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Dynamic Routing & Agent Coordination: Details routing mechanics, specialized domain dispatch, and task coordination", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Routing to specialized domain agents", "Parallel dispatch for independent tasks", "Sequential execution for dependent steps"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Ambiguity Handling & Fallback Retries: Covers confidence thresholds, clarification questions, and safe fallbacks", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Confidence thresholding triggering clarification questions", "Graceful fallback to default handlers or human queues", "Retry policies with exponential backoff on failure"]}], "critical_failures": ["Cannot explain how ambiguous or multi-part queries are handled without failing", "Suggests hardcoded keyword matching as the sole routing mechanism in complex agent systems"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-016"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'For agentic AI applications, what are the metrics you are testing?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "AI Systems Ops: Evaluation, Guardrails & Observability", "subcategory": "Evaluation & Observability", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "Evaluating agentic AI requires multi-tier metrics and distributed observability. Component-level metrics evaluate intent routing accuracy, tool selection precision/recall, and parameter schema validity. Workflow-level metrics track end-to-end task success rate, execution trajectory efficiency (step count), latency, and cost per task. Observability is achieved through distributed tracing (e.g., OpenTelemetry, LangSmith) capturing hierarchical spans across agent reasoning hops, tool execution latency, and model token usage for debugging silent failures.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Agent Roles & Coordination", "weight": 40, "description": "Explains agent roles, supervisor/orchestrator responsibilities, and routing mechanisms."}, {"name": "State & Communication", "weight": 30, "description": "Details shared state management, tool usage, and inter-agent message passing."}, {"name": "Validation & Fallback", "weight": 30, "description": "Covers tool validation, retry mechanisms, and human-in-the-loop escalation."}], "critical_failures": ["Describes a single monolithic LLM prompt without agent decomposition or state routing.", "Fails to specify how subagents coordinate or pass execution state."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-017"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'From an agentic AI perspective what are the projects that you worked on?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Engineering Leadership & Experience", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "CAREER_PROGRESSION_OWNERSHIP", "reference_answer": "Candidate articulates hands-on engineering experience designing, implementing, and deploying agentic AI systems. Discusses personal technical ownership across prompt engineering, tool integration, orchestration frameworks (e.g., LangGraph), state persistence, and evaluation pipelines. Shares realistic production challenges: debugging non-deterministic failures, mitigating tool calling errors, managing context windows, and iterating from POC prototypes to production-grade deployments with monitoring and guardrails.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Role Scope & Ownership", "weight": 35, "description": "Describes clear responsibilities, architecture ownership, and system impact in recent roles."}, {"name": "Technical & Cross-functional Leadership", "weight": 35, "description": "Explains engineering decisions, collaboration with stakeholders, and technical leadership."}, {"name": "System Scale & Complexity", "weight": 30, "description": "Details scale, throughput, reliability requirements, or AI integration scope of systems owned."}], "critical_failures": ["Vague description of personal role without specific technical contributions or ownership.", "Unable to explain architectural decisions or system scope in previous work."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-018"}', 1),
