 sub-tasks with dependencies"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Dynamic Routing & Agent Coordination: Details routing mechanics, specialized domain dispatch, and task coordination", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Routing to specialized domain agents", "Parallel dispatch for independent tasks", "Sequential execution for dependent steps"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Ambiguity Handling & Fallback Retries: Covers confidence thresholds, clarification questions, and safe fallbacks", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Confidence thresholding triggering clarification questions", "Graceful fallback to default handlers or human queues", "Retry policies with exponential backoff on failure"]}], "critical_failures": ["Cannot explain how ambiguous or multi-part queries are handled without failing", "Suggests hardcoded keyword matching as the sole routing mechanism in complex agent systems"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-146"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What role do guardrail agents play in an agentic AI system, and how do they help protect sensitive data?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "AI Systems Ops: Evaluation, Guardrails & Observability", "subcategory": "Agent Safety & Governance", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "SAFETY_GUARDRAILS_HITL", "reference_answer": "Agent safety requires architectural defense-in-depth: soft conversational guidance resides in system prompts, while hard invariants (rate limiting, permissions, schema validation, transaction boundaries) must be enforced deterministically in code. Input and output guardrails intercept prompts to block jailbreaks, prompt injection, and PII leaks. For high-impact, non-reversible actions (e.g., database writes, payments), an asynchronous human-in-the-loop (HITL) gate pauses execution until an authorized human operator approves or rejects the action.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Architectural Boundaries (Code vs Prompt): Draws clear boundaries between deterministic code guardrails and prompt instructions", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Deterministic code for security, access control, and validation", "Prompts for tone, domain guidance, and reasoning nuance", "Defense-in-depth architecture"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Human-in-the-Loop & Escalation: Details HITL triggers, execution pausing, audit trails, and human approval handoff", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Pre-execution interruption for high-stakes actions", "Audit trail logging of agent proposals and human decisions", "Escalation routing and timeout handling"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Threat Defense & Compliance: Covers prompt injection detection, PII sanitization, and recursion breakers", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["PII redaction and tokenization", "Prompt injection and jailbreak detection", "Step limits and timeout breakers to prevent infinite execution loops"]}], "critical_failures": ["Relies solely on prompt instructions to enforce critical security or financial constraints", "Unaware of human-in-the-loop patterns or escalation mechanisms"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-147"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What role does an orchestration layer play in routing queries to domain-specific agents?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Routing & Planning", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ROUTING_AND_PLANNING", "reference_answer": "Routing and planning govern how queries are classified, decomposed, and executed. The router performs intent classification using semantic embeddings, few-shot classifiers, or fast small LLMs with structured output schemas. Complex queries are decomposed by a planner agent into directed sub-tasks with dependency graphs. Ambiguous intents trigger interactive clarification turns rather than hallucinations, while unrecognized queries route to fallback domain agents or human escalation queues.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Intent Classification & Task Decomposition: Explains intent detection strategies and decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Semantic embedding search or few-shot LLM classification", "Structured JSON output schema for routing tags", "Decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks with dependencies"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Dynamic Routing & Agent Coordination: Details routing mechanics, specialized domain dispatch, and task coordination", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Routing to specialized domain agents", "Parallel dispatch for independent tasks", "Sequential execution for dependent steps"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Ambiguity Handling & Fallback Retries: Covers confidence thresholds, clarification questions, and safe fallbacks", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Confidence thresholding triggering clarification questions", "Graceful fallback to default handlers or human queues", "Retry policies with exponential backoff on failure"]}], "critical_failures": ["Cannot explain how ambiguous or multi-part queries are handled without failing", "Suggests hardcoded keyword matching as the sole routing mechanism in complex agent systems"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-148"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What role would generative and agentic AI systems play in solving key challenges in the home healthcare ecosystem?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "System Architecture & Design", "subcategory": "Domain Workflow Automation", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "Domain-specific agentic automation decomposes unstructured, multi-step business workflows into specialized agent roles with domain tools. In recruiting or compliance workflows, dedicated agents handle parsing, criteria evaluation, bias auditing, and decision support with audit logging. To maintain enterprise trust and compliance, autonomous agents provide recommendations with structured rationale and citations, while irreversible decisions require human review.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Agent Roles & Coordination", "weight": 40, "description": "Explains agent roles, supervisor/orchestrator responsibilities, and routing mechanisms."}, {"name": "State & Communication", "weight": 30, "description": "Details shared state management, tool usage, and inter-agent message passing."}, {"name": "Validation & Fallback", "weight": 30, "description": "Covers tool validation, retry mechanisms, and human-in-the-loop escalation."}], "critical_failures": ["Describes a single monolithic LLM prompt without agent decomposition or state routing.", "Fails to specify how subagents coordinate or pass execution state."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-149"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What roles and responsibilities would you assign to different agents in such a system, and how would they interact?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Multi-Agent System Architecture", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "The system employs a hierarchical supervisor-worker multi-agent architecture to manage complex domain tasks. An Orchestrator/Supervisor agent maintains global workflow state, performs intent classification, and delegates domain sub-tasks to specialized domain agents (e.g., Knowledge Agent, Transaction Agent, Policy Agent). Agents communicate via structured schemas over a shared state graph. Tools are decoupled from reasoning, exposed as typed functions with strict parameter validation. Resilience is ensured through self-reflection loops for transient errors, recursion depth limits to prevent infinite execution cycles, and escalation paths to human operators.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Multi-Agent Roles & Architecture: Defines clear agent roles, responsibilities, and separation of concerns across domain boundaries", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Supervisor / Orchestrator coordination pattern", "Specialized domain workers with dedicated tools", "Decoupling reasoning from execution"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Coordination, State & Communication: Explains how state and message payloads flow between supervisor and domain agents", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Shared state dictionary and context passing", "Structured input/output contracts between agents", "Parallel vs sequential sub-task execution"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Validation, Fallback & Escalation: Covers error handling, infinite loop prevention, guardrail validation, and human handoff", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Step/depth limits (max iterations)", "Output validation and reflection loops", "Graceful fallback and human escalation"]}], "critical_failures": ["Designs a monolithic prompt instead of specialized agent roles", "Lacks mechanisms to prevent infinite agent execution loops"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-150"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What specific measurable outcome did the code review agents achieve in terms of improving developer productivity?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Python & Data Manipulation", "subcategory": "AI-Assisted Software Engineering", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "CODING_DEVELOPER_PRODUCTIVITY", "reference_answer": "AI coding assistants and companion agents (e.g. Cursor, Copilot, automated PR review agents) accelerate development through context-aware autocompletion, boilerplate generation, AST-aware code navigation, and test generation. To prevent regressions, outputs must undergo strict deterministic verification: syntax linting, static analysis, unit test execution, and human code review. Measurable impact is tracked through reduced PR review turnaround time, test coverage improvements, and developer velocity while mitigating risks around hallucinated APIs and security vulnerabilities.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Workflow Integration & Tooling: Explains how coding agents integrate into development environments (IDEs, CI/CD PR bots, AST tools)", "weight": 40, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Integration with IDEs or GitHub PR workflows", "Automating boilerplate, docstrings, or unit tests", "Repository indexing and AST context retrieval"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Verification & Quality Assurance: Details verification safeguards before committing AI-generated code", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Mandatory unit test execution and static analysis", "Human-in-the-loop code review approval", "Catching security vulnerabilities or hallucinated APIs"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Productivity Metrics & Operational Limitations: Discusses measurable productivity gains and awareness of tool limitations", "weight": 25, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Tracking metrics like PR turnaround time or test coverage", "Handling context window limits in large mono-repos", "Addressing licensing, privacy, and code IP concerns"]}], "critical_failures": ["Advocates blindly merging AI-generated code without manual review or automated testing", "Cannot explain how coding agents assist software engineering beyond basic string autocompletion"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-151"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What strategies did you use to prevent infinite loops or uncontrolled iterations in multi-agent systems?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "AI Systems Ops: Evaluation, Guardrails & Observability", "subcategory": "Agent Safety & Governance", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "SAFETY_GUARDRAILS_HITL", "reference_answer": "Agent safety requires architectural defense-in-depth: soft conversational guidance resides in system prompts, while hard invariants (rate limiting, permissions, schema validation, transaction boundaries) must be enforced deterministically in code. Input and output guardrails intercept prompts to block jailbreaks, prompt injection, and PII leaks. For high-impact, non-reversible actions (e.g., database writes, payments), an asynchronous human-in-the-loop (HITL) gate pauses execution until an authorized human operator approves or rejects the action.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Architectural Boundaries (Code vs Prompt): Draws clear boundaries between deterministic code guardrails and prompt instructions", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Deterministic code for security, access control, and validation", "Prompts for tone, domain guidance, and reasoning nuance", "Defense-in-depth architecture"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Human-in-the-Loop & Escalation: Details HITL triggers, execution pausing, audit trails, and human approval handoff", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Pre-execution interruption for high-stakes actions", "Audit trail logging of agent proposals and human decisions", "Escalation routing and timeout handling"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Threat Defense & Compliance: Covers prompt injection detection, PII sanitization, and recursion breakers", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["PII redaction and tokenization", "Prompt injection and jailbreak detection", "Step limits and timeout breakers to prevent infinite execution loops"]}], "critical_failures": ["Relies solely on prompt instructions to enforce critical security or financial constraints", "Unaware of human-in-the-loop patterns or escalation mechanisms"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-152"}', 1),
