('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What was your experience with Model Context Protocol (MCP), and how did you use it for tool integration?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Model Context Protocol & Tools", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "TOOL_INTEGRATION_MCP", "reference_answer": "Model Context Protocol (MCP) standardizes how models discover, authorize, and invoke external resources and tools via JSON-RPC 2.0 (over stdio or SSE). It decouples client agent reasoning from server-side tool execution, providing standardized schemas, access control, and isolation. Trade-offs in production include RPC latency, authorization overhead, and debugging complexity compared to native direct function calls.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "MCP Architecture & Transport: Explains client-server decoupling, JSON-RPC 2.0, and dynamic tool discovery", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Decouples model host/client from tool server", "Uses JSON-RPC 2.0 via stdio or SSE/HTTP", "Dynamic schema discovery without hardcoding"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Security, Authorization & Schemas: Details typed JSON schemas, role-based authorization, and isolated tool execution", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Typed JSON schema validation for parameters", "Role-based authorization and tool permissions (RBAC)", "Execution isolation and sandboxing"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Production Trade-offs & Adoption: Evaluates latency overhead, enterprise readiness, and comparison with direct function calls", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["RPC overhead and network latency", "Standardization across multiple agents vs custom code", "Observability, distributed tracing, and error handling"]}], "critical_failures": ["Confuses MCP with a proprietary model architecture rather than a protocol", "Claims MCP eliminates the need for parameter schema validation"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-158"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What were the dynamic routing and dynamic decisioning components of the agentic piece in this workflow?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Routing & Planning", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ROUTING_AND_PLANNING", "reference_answer": "Routing and planning govern how queries are classified, decomposed, and executed. The router performs intent classification using semantic embeddings, few-shot classifiers, or fast small LLMs with structured output schemas. Complex queries are decomposed by a planner agent into directed sub-tasks with dependency graphs. Ambiguous intents trigger interactive clarification turns rather than hallucinations, while unrecognized queries route to fallback domain agents or human escalation queues.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Intent Classification & Task Decomposition: Explains intent detection strategies and decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Semantic embedding search or few-shot LLM classification", "Structured JSON output schema for routing tags", "Decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks with dependencies"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Dynamic Routing & Agent Coordination: Details routing mechanics, specialized domain dispatch, and task coordination", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Routing to specialized domain agents", "Parallel dispatch for independent tasks", "Sequential execution for dependent steps"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Ambiguity Handling & Fallback Retries: Covers confidence thresholds, clarification questions, and safe fallbacks", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Confidence thresholding triggering clarification questions", "Graceful fallback to default handlers or human queues", "Retry policies with exponential backoff on failure"]}], "critical_failures": ["Cannot explain how ambiguous or multi-part queries are handled without failing", "Suggests hardcoded keyword matching as the sole routing mechanism in complex agent systems"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-159"}', 1),
