o sub-tasks with dependencies"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Dynamic Routing & Agent Coordination: Details routing mechanics, specialized domain dispatch, and task coordination", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Routing to specialized domain agents", "Parallel dispatch for independent tasks", "Sequential execution for dependent steps"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Ambiguity Handling & Fallback Retries: Covers confidence thresholds, clarification questions, and safe fallbacks", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Confidence thresholding triggering clarification questions", "Graceful fallback to default handlers or human queues", "Retry policies with exponential backoff on failure"]}], "critical_failures": ["Cannot explain how ambiguous or multi-part queries are handled without failing", "Suggests hardcoded keyword matching as the sole routing mechanism in complex agent systems"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-160"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What''s your experience using coding agents? Coding what environment do you use right now? What IDE? What sort of environment do you use?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Python & Data Manipulation", "subcategory": "AI-Assisted Software Engineering", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "CODING_DEVELOPER_PRODUCTIVITY", "reference_answer": "AI coding assistants and companion agents (e.g. Cursor, Copilot, automated PR review agents) accelerate development through context-aware autocompletion, boilerplate generation, AST-aware code navigation, and test generation. To prevent regressions, outputs must undergo strict deterministic verification: syntax linting, static analysis, unit test execution, and human code review. Measurable impact is tracked through reduced PR review turnaround time, test coverage improvements, and developer velocity while mitigating risks around hallucinated APIs and security vulnerabilities.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Workflow Integration & Tooling: Explains how coding agents integrate into development environments (IDEs, CI/CD PR bots, AST tools)", "weight": 40, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Integration with IDEs or GitHub PR workflows", "Automating boilerplate, docstrings, or unit tests", "Repository indexing and AST context retrieval"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Verification & Quality Assurance: Details verification safeguards before committing AI-generated code", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Mandatory unit test execution and static analysis", "Human-in-the-loop code review approval", "Catching security vulnerabilities or hallucinated APIs"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Productivity Metrics & Operational Limitations: Discusses measurable productivity gains and awareness of tool limitations", "weight": 25, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Tracking metrics like PR turnaround time or test coverage", "Handling context window limits in large mono-repos", "Addressing licensing, privacy, and code IP concerns"]}], "critical_failures": ["Advocates blindly merging AI-generated code without manual review or automated testing", "Cannot explain how coding agents assist software engineering beyond basic string autocompletion"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-161"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'When should agent-based systems be used versus traditional deterministic systems?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Architectural Trade-offs & Comparisons", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "Agentic architectures introduce autonomy, dynamic tool execution, and flexible problem-solving, but at the cost of non-determinism, multi-second latency, token expenditure, and debugging complexity. Deterministic software or static linear pipelines are preferred for exact arithmetic, ACID compliance, low-latency APIs, and fixed sequential business logic. When scaling from single-agent to multi-agent, single agents suffer from prompt bloat and tool confusion, while multi-agent systems provide clear separation of concerns at the cost of orchestration latency.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Agent Roles & Coordination", "weight": 40, "description": "Explains agent roles, supervisor/orchestrator responsibilities, and routing mechanisms."}, {"name": "State & Communication", "weight": 30, "description": "Details shared state management, tool usage, and inter-agent message passing."}, {"name": "Validation & Fallback", "weight": 30, "description": "Covers tool validation, retry mechanisms, and human-in-the-loop escalation."}], "critical_failures": ["Describes a single monolithic LLM prompt without agent decomposition or state routing.", "Fails to specify how subagents coordinate or pass execution state."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-162"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Generative AI & LLMs', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'Which areas within AI/ML are you most interested in developing further, particularly in relation to generative AI, NLP, or agent-based systems?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Multi-Agent System Architecture", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "The system employs a hierarchical supervisor-worker multi-agent architecture to manage complex domain tasks. An Orchestrator/Supervisor agent maintains global workflow state, performs intent classification, and delegates domain sub-tasks to specialized domain agents (e.g., Knowledge Agent, Transaction Agent, Policy Agent). Agents communicate via structured schemas over a shared state graph. Tools are decoupled from reasoning, exposed as typed functions with strict parameter validation. Resilience is ensured through self-reflection loops for transient errors, recursion depth limits to prevent infinite execution cycles, and escalation paths to human operators.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Multi-Agent Roles & Architecture: Defines clear agent roles, responsibilities, and separation of concerns across domain boundaries", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Supervisor / Orchestrator coordination pattern", "Specialized domain workers with dedicated tools", "Decoupling reasoning from execution"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Coordination, State & Communication: Explains how state and message payloads flow between supervisor and domain agents", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Shared state dictionary and context passing", "Structured input/output contracts between agents", "Parallel vs sequential sub-task execution"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Validation, Fallback & Escalation: Covers error handling, infinite loop prevention, guardrail validation, and human handoff", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Step/depth limits (max iterations)", "Output validation and reflection loops", "Graceful fallback and human escalation"]}], "critical_failures": ["Designs a monolithic prompt instead of specialized agent roles", "Lacks mechanisms to prevent infinite agent execution loops"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-163"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'Why are you interested in Actura AI and its healthcare AI orchestration platform?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "System Architecture & Design", "subcategory": "Domain Workflow Automation", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "Domain-specific agentic automation decomposes unstructured, multi-step business workflows into specialized agent roles with domain tools. In recruiting or compliance workflows, dedicated agents handle parsing, criteria evaluation, bias auditing, and decision support with audit logging. To maintain enterprise trust and compliance, autonomous agents provide recommendations with structured rationale and citations, while irreversible decisions require human review.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Agent Roles & Coordination", "weight": 40, "description": "Explains agent roles, supervisor/orchestrator responsibilities, and routing mechanisms."}, {"name": "State & Communication", "weight": 30, "description": "Details shared state management, tool usage, and inter-agent message passing."}, {"name": "Validation & Fallback", "weight": 30, "description": "Covers tool validation, retry mechanisms, and human-in-the-loop escalation."}], "critical_failures": ["Describes a single monolithic LLM prompt without agent decomposition or state routing.", "Fails to specify how subagents coordinate or pass execution state."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-164"}', 1),
