('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'HARD', 'SPECIFIC', 120, 'How did you design and implement your agentic system, including orchestration, routing, and tool usage?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Routing & Planning", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ROUTING_AND_PLANNING", "reference_answer": "Routing and planning govern how queries are classified, decomposed, and executed. The router performs intent classification using semantic embeddings, few-shot classifiers, or fast small LLMs with structured output schemas. Complex queries are decomposed by a planner agent into directed sub-tasks with dependency graphs. Ambiguous intents trigger interactive clarification turns rather than hallucinations, while unrecognized queries route to fallback domain agents or human escalation queues.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Intent Classification & Task Decomposition: Explains intent detection strategies and decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Semantic embedding search or few-shot LLM classification", "Structured JSON output schema for routing tags", "Decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks with dependencies"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Dynamic Routing & Agent Coordination: Details routing mechanics, specialized domain dispatch, and task coordination", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Routing to specialized domain agents", "Parallel dispatch for independent tasks", "Sequential execution for dependent steps"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Ambiguity Handling & Fallback Retries: Covers confidence thresholds, clarification questions, and safe fallbacks", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Confidence thresholding triggering clarification questions", "Graceful fallback to default handlers or human queues", "Retry policies with exponential backoff on failure"]}], "critical_failures": ["Cannot explain how ambiguous or multi-part queries are handled without failing", "Suggests hardcoded keyword matching as the sole routing mechanism in complex agent systems"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-194"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'Software Engineering', 'Databases & Caching', 'HARD', 'SPECIFIC', 120, 'How did you design and improve a text-to-SQL agent, including handling accuracy issues and incorporating metadata?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Databases & Caching", "subcategory": "Database & Text-to-SQL Agents", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "TEXT_TO_SQL_AND_DATA", "reference_answer": "Text-to-SQL and data agents operate through a Generate-Validate-Execute-Reflect pattern. 1. Dynamic Schema Ingestion: Relevant table schemas, DDL, and sample foreign keys are retrieved via vector search to protect context window limits. 2. Static Validation: An AST parser (e.g., sqlglot) verifies syntax and strictly enforces read-only SELECT constraints. 3. Sandboxed Execution: Queries run under least-privilege read-only roles with query timeouts. 4. Reflection Loop: Database execution exceptions are fed back into the reasoning loop for iterative self-healing.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Schema Context & Prompt Engineering: Explains dynamic DDL pruning and context assembly without context window overflow", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Dynamic schema retrieval using semantic search over DDL", "Injecting foreign key relationships and few-shot exemplars", "Managing token budgets with large enterprise schemas"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "AST Parsing & Query Safety: Covers query validation, SQL injection prevention, and read-only enforcement", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["AST validation (e.g., sqlglot) to block destructive queries (DROP, DELETE)", "Enforcing read-only permissions and row LIMIT clauses", "Preventing SQL injection through parameterization"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Execution & Reflection Self-Correction: Details sandboxed execution and self-healing error correction loops", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Executing against read-only replicas with query timeouts", "Feeding database error messages back into the model to self-correct", "Fallback to human data analyst on persistent failures"]}], "critical_failures": ["Allows raw unvalidated SQL generation with write permissions directly against production databases", "Dumps the entire monolithic database schema into the prompt without pruning"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-195"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How did you design the multi-agent code review system, and what was the purpose of different agent personas?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Python & Data Manipulation", "subcategory": "AI-Assisted Software Engineering", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "AI coding assistants and companion agents (e.g. Cursor, Copilot, automated PR review agents) accelerate development through context-aware autocompletion, boilerplate generation, AST-aware code navigation, and test generation. To prevent regressions, outputs must undergo strict deterministic verification: syntax linting, static analysis, unit test execution, and human code review. Measurable impact is tracked through reduced PR review turnaround time, test coverage improvements, and developer velocity while mitigating risks around hallucinated APIs and security vulnerabilities.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Agent Roles & Coordination", "weight": 40, "description": "Explains agent roles, supervisor/orchestrator responsibilities, and routing mechanisms."}, {"name": "State & Communication", "weight": 30, "description": "Details shared state management, tool usage, and inter-agent message passing."}, {"name": "Validation & Fallback", "weight": 30, "description": "Covers tool validation, retry mechanisms, and human-in-the-loop escalation."}], "critical_failures": ["Describes a single monolithic LLM prompt without agent decomposition or state routing.", "Fails to specify how subagents coordinate or pass execution state."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-196"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'HARD', 'SPECIFIC', 120, 'How did you design the multi-agent system, and what roles do different agents such as policy, claims, and router agents play?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Routing & Planning", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ROUTING_AND_PLANNING", "reference_answer": "Routing and planning govern how queries are classified, decomposed, and executed. The router performs intent classification using semantic embeddings, few-shot classifiers, or fast small LLMs with structured output schemas. Complex queries are decomposed by a planner agent into directed sub-tasks with dependency graphs. Ambiguous intents trigger interactive clarification turns rather than hallucinations, while unrecognized queries route to fallback domain agents or human escalation queues.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Intent Classification & Task Decomposition: Explains intent detection strategies and decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Semantic embedding search or few-shot LLM classification", "Structured JSON output schema for routing tags", "Decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks with dependencies"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Dynamic Routing & Agent Coordination: Details routing mechanics, specialized domain dispatch, and task coordination", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Routing to specialized domain agents", "Parallel dispatch for independent tasks", "Sequential execution for dependent steps"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Ambiguity Handling & Fallback Retries: Covers confidence thresholds, clarification questions, and safe fallbacks", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Confidence thresholding triggering clarification questions", "Graceful fallback to default handlers or human queues", "Retry policies with exponential backoff on failure"]}], "critical_failures": ["Cannot explain how ambiguous or multi-part queries are handled without failing", "Suggests hardcoded keyword matching as the sole routing mechanism in complex agent systems"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-197"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'HARD', 'SPECIFIC', 120, 'How did you design the multi-agent system, including orchestration, supervisor agents, memory management, and tool calling?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Models & Context Engineering", "subcategory": "Memory & Context Engineering", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "MEMORY_AND_STATE", "reference_answer": "Production agent memory separates short-term working context (active scratchpad within the model''s context window) from long-term persistent storage. Short-term context is optimized using sliding windows, token budgets, and recursive summarization to prevent attention dilution and token exhaustion. Long-term memory persists episodic conversation history and extracted entities (e.g., claim/ticket IDs) in Redis or Vector/Relational databases, retrieving them dynamically via similarity or structured metadata queries.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Short-Term vs Long-Term Memory Tiering: Differentiates short-term working context from persistent long-term storage", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Short-term context window buffer vs external database persistence", "Redis for fast session cache", "Vector or relational databases for persistent entity memory"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Entity Extraction & Context Injection: Explains entity extraction, indexing, and dynamic context injection techniques", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Structured entity extraction (e.g., claim numbers, user IDs)", "Dynamic injection into system prompts or tool parameters", "Context relevance filtering"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Context Window Optimization & Privacy: Covers context window management, compaction, and privacy/retention policies", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Sliding window buffering and summarization", "PII sanitization before persisting to long-term storage", "TTL and cache invalidation policies"]}], "critical_failures": ["Claims the entire raw chat history should be injected into every prompt indefinitely", "Cannot distinguish between in-memory session state and persistent storage"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-198"}', 1),
