35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Supervisor / Orchestrator coordination pattern", "Specialized domain workers with dedicated tools", "Decoupling reasoning from execution"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Coordination, State & Communication: Explains how state and message payloads flow between supervisor and domain agents", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Shared state dictionary and context passing", "Structured input/output contracts between agents", "Parallel vs sequential sub-task execution"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Validation, Fallback & Escalation: Covers error handling, infinite loop prevention, guardrail validation, and human handoff", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Step/depth limits (max iterations)", "Output validation and reflection loops", "Graceful fallback and human escalation"]}], "critical_failures": ["Designs a monolithic prompt instead of specialized agent roles", "Lacks mechanisms to prevent infinite agent execution loops"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-199"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'HARD', 'SPECIFIC', 120, 'How did you detect and prevent infinite loops in agent workflows, and what safeguards did you implement?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "AI Systems Ops: Evaluation, Guardrails & Observability", "subcategory": "Agent Safety & Governance", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "SAFETY_GUARDRAILS_HITL", "reference_answer": "Agent safety requires architectural defense-in-depth: soft conversational guidance resides in system prompts, while hard invariants (rate limiting, permissions, schema validation, transaction boundaries) must be enforced deterministically in code. Input and output guardrails intercept prompts to block jailbreaks, prompt injection, and PII leaks. For high-impact, non-reversible actions (e.g., database writes, payments), an asynchronous human-in-the-loop (HITL) gate pauses execution until an authorized human operator approves or rejects the action.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Architectural Boundaries (Code vs Prompt): Draws clear boundaries between deterministic code guardrails and prompt instructions", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Deterministic code for security, access control, and validation", "Prompts for tone, domain guidance, and reasoning nuance", "Defense-in-depth architecture"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Human-in-the-Loop & Escalation: Details HITL triggers, execution pausing, audit trails, and human approval handoff", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Pre-execution interruption for high-stakes actions", "Audit trail logging of agent proposals and human decisions", "Escalation routing and timeout handling"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Threat Defense & Compliance: Covers prompt injection detection, PII sanitization, and recursion breakers", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["PII redaction and tokenization", "Prompt injection and jailbreak detection", "Step limits and timeout breakers to prevent infinite execution loops"]}], "critical_failures": ["Relies solely on prompt instructions to enforce critical security or financial constraints", "Unaware of human-in-the-loop patterns or escalation mechanisms"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-200"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How did you determine that prompt engineering alone was insufficient, and why did you adopt orchestration frameworks like LangGraph?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "LangGraph & Stateful Orchestration", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "LANGGRAPH_ORCHESTRATION", "reference_answer": "LangGraph models agent workflows as cyclic computational graphs using StateGraph, nodes, and conditional edges. Nodes represent compute steps or agent reasoning tasks, while conditional routing functions determine dynamic transitions based on state. State is persisted across cycles and multi-turn conversations using thread-level checkpointers (e.g. Postgres, SQLite, Redis), enabling resumable workflows, human-in-the-loop interrupts, and rollback capabilities.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Graph Architecture & Control Flow: Explains StateGraph, nodes, edges, conditional routing, and cyclic execution", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Nodes representing agents or tools", "Conditional edges for dynamic runtime routing", "Cyclic execution loops vs linear DAGs"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "State Schema & Reducers: Details typed state schema, message passing, and state reducer functions", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Typed state dictionary (TypedDict / Pydantic)", "State reducers (e.g., add_messages) for combining updates", "Maintaining conversation history and tool results in graph state"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Persistence & Human-in-the-Loop: Covers checkpointing, human-in-the-loop interrupts, and failure recovery", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Checkpointer persistence (Postgres, SQLite, Redis)", "Interrupting before/after nodes for human approval", "State rollback, branching, and time-travel debugging"]}], "critical_failures": ["Asserts LangGraph is strictly a linear pipeline identical to basic LangChain chains", "Cannot explain how state is updated or passed between graph nodes"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-201"}', 1),
