ries", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Supervisor / Orchestrator coordination pattern", "Specialized domain workers with dedicated tools", "Decoupling reasoning from execution"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Coordination, State & Communication: Explains how state and message payloads flow between supervisor and domain agents", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Shared state dictionary and context passing", "Structured input/output contracts between agents", "Parallel vs sequential sub-task execution"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Validation, Fallback & Escalation: Covers error handling, infinite loop prevention, guardrail validation, and human handoff", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Step/depth limits (max iterations)", "Output validation and reflection loops", "Graceful fallback and human escalation"]}], "critical_failures": ["Designs a monolithic prompt instead of specialized agent roles", "Lacks mechanisms to prevent infinite agent execution loops"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-215"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'HARD', 'SPECIFIC', 120, 'How do you design and orchestrate multi-agent workflows, including the role of router agents and task-specific agents?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Routing & Planning", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ROUTING_AND_PLANNING", "reference_answer": "Routing and planning govern how queries are classified, decomposed, and executed. The router performs intent classification using semantic embeddings, few-shot classifiers, or fast small LLMs with structured output schemas. Complex queries are decomposed by a planner agent into directed sub-tasks with dependency graphs. Ambiguous intents trigger interactive clarification turns rather than hallucinations, while unrecognized queries route to fallback domain agents or human escalation queues.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Intent Classification & Task Decomposition: Explains intent detection strategies and decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Semantic embedding search or few-shot LLM classification", "Structured JSON output schema for routing tags", "Decomposing complex queries into sub-tasks with dependencies"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Dynamic Routing & Agent Coordination: Details routing mechanics, specialized domain dispatch, and task coordination", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Routing to specialized domain agents", "Parallel dispatch for independent tasks", "Sequential execution for dependent steps"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Ambiguity Handling & Fallback Retries: Covers confidence thresholds, clarification questions, and safe fallbacks", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Confidence thresholding triggering clarification questions", "Graceful fallback to default handlers or human queues", "Retry policies with exponential backoff on failure"]}], "critical_failures": ["Cannot explain how ambiguous or multi-part queries are handled without failing", "Suggests hardcoded keyword matching as the sole routing mechanism in complex agent systems"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-216"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How do you design and orchestrate multiple agents within a complex agentic AI application?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Multi-Agent System Architecture", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "The system employs a hierarchical supervisor-worker multi-agent architecture to manage complex domain tasks. An Orchestrator/Supervisor agent maintains global workflow state, performs intent classification, and delegates domain sub-tasks to specialized domain agents (e.g., Knowledge Agent, Transaction Agent, Policy Agent). Agents communicate via structured schemas over a shared state graph. Tools are decoupled from reasoning, exposed as typed functions with strict parameter validation. Resilience is ensured through self-reflection loops for transient errors, recursion depth limits to prevent infinite execution cycles, and escalation paths to human operators.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Multi-Agent Roles & Architecture: Defines clear agent roles, responsibilities, and separation of concerns across domain boundaries", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Supervisor / Orchestrator coordination pattern", "Specialized domain workers with dedicated tools", "Decoupling reasoning from execution"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Coordination, State & Communication: Explains how state and message payloads flow between supervisor and domain agents", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Shared state dictionary and context passing", "Structured input/output contracts between agents", "Parallel vs sequential sub-task execution"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Validation, Fallback & Escalation: Covers error handling, infinite loop prevention, guardrail validation, and human handoff", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Step/depth limits (max iterations)", "Output validation and reflection loops", "Graceful fallback and human escalation"]}], "critical_failures": ["Designs a monolithic prompt instead of specialized agent roles", "Lacks mechanisms to prevent infinite agent execution loops"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-217"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'AI Systems Ops: Evaluation, Guardrails & Observability', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How do you design evaluation pipelines that convert agent traces into actionable insights for system improvement?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "AI Systems Ops: Evaluation, Guardrails & Observability", "subcategory": "Evaluation & Observability", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "EVALUATION_OBSERVABILITY", "reference_answer": "Evaluating agentic AI requires multi-tier metrics and distributed observability. Component-level metrics evaluate intent routing accuracy, tool selection precision/recall, and parameter schema validity. Workflow-level metrics track end-to-end task success rate, execution trajectory efficiency (step count), latency, and cost per task. Observability is achieved through distributed tracing (e.g., OpenTelemetry, LangSmith) capturing hierarchical spans across agent reasoning hops, tool execution latency, and model token usage for debugging silent failures.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Evaluation Metrics & Benchmarks: Identifies component-level and workflow-level evaluation metrics", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["End-to-end task completion rate", "Tool selection accuracy and parameter validity", "Trajectory step efficiency, latency, and cost tracking"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Distributed Tracing & Observability: Explains distributed tracing infrastructure across agent reasoning hops and tools", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Hierarchical spans (Agent -> Node -> Tool)", "OpenTelemetry / LangSmith tracing integration", "Capturing inputs, intermediate thoughts, and tool return payloads"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Feedback Loops & Continuous Improvement: Details golden evaluation datasets, LLM-as-a-judge, and feedback loop integration", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Curated golden evaluation datasets representing edge cases", "Calibrated LLM-as-a-judge for semantic verification", "Continuous prompt and workflow iteration based on production traces"]}], "critical_failures": ["Relies solely on perplexity or BLEU scores to evaluate multi-step agent systems", "Lacks understanding of distributed tracing or step-by-step trajectory debugging"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-218"}', 1),
