e, "acceptable_evidence": ["Supervisor / Orchestrator coordination pattern", "Specialized domain workers with dedicated tools", "Decoupling reasoning from execution"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Coordination, State & Communication: Explains how state and message payloads flow between supervisor and domain agents", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Shared state dictionary and context passing", "Structured input/output contracts between agents", "Parallel vs sequential sub-task execution"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Validation, Fallback & Escalation: Covers error handling, infinite loop prevention, guardrail validation, and human handoff", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Step/depth limits (max iterations)", "Output validation and reflection loops", "Graceful fallback and human escalation"]}], "critical_failures": ["Designs a monolithic prompt instead of specialized agent roles", "Lacks mechanisms to prevent infinite agent execution loops"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-224"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'NLP', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How do you implement a stateful chat agent that maintains conversation history and uses it for generating contextual responses?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Models & Context Engineering", "subcategory": "Memory & Context Engineering", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "MEMORY_AND_STATE", "reference_answer": "Production agent memory separates short-term working context (active scratchpad within the model''s context window) from long-term persistent storage. Short-term context is optimized using sliding windows, token budgets, and recursive summarization to prevent attention dilution and token exhaustion. Long-term memory persists episodic conversation history and extracted entities (e.g., claim/ticket IDs) in Redis or Vector/Relational databases, retrieving them dynamically via similarity or structured metadata queries.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Short-Term vs Long-Term Memory Tiering: Differentiates short-term working context from persistent long-term storage", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Short-term context window buffer vs external database persistence", "Redis for fast session cache", "Vector or relational databases for persistent entity memory"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Entity Extraction & Context Injection: Explains entity extraction, indexing, and dynamic context injection techniques", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Structured entity extraction (e.g., claim numbers, user IDs)", "Dynamic injection into system prompts or tool parameters", "Context relevance filtering"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Context Window Optimization & Privacy: Covers context window management, compaction, and privacy/retention policies", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Sliding window buffering and summarization", "PII sanitization before persisting to long-term storage", "TTL and cache invalidation policies"]}], "critical_failures": ["Claims the entire raw chat history should be injected into every prompt indefinitely", "Cannot distinguish between in-memory session state and persistent storage"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-225"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How do you implement and manage parallel tool calls versus sequential or multi-step tool workflows?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Architectural Trade-offs & Comparisons", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "Agentic architectures introduce autonomy, dynamic tool execution, and flexible problem-solving, but at the cost of non-determinism, multi-second latency, token expenditure, and debugging complexity. Deterministic software or static linear pipelines are preferred for exact arithmetic, ACID compliance, low-latency APIs, and fixed sequential business logic. When scaling from single-agent to multi-agent, single agents suffer from prompt bloat and tool confusion, while multi-agent systems provide clear separation of concerns at the cost of orchestration latency.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Agent Roles & Coordination", "weight": 40, "description": "Explains agent roles, supervisor/orchestrator responsibilities, and routing mechanisms."}, {"name": "State & Communication", "weight": 30, "description": "Details shared state management, tool usage, and inter-agent message passing."}, {"name": "Validation & Fallback", "weight": 30, "description": "Covers tool validation, retry mechanisms, and human-in-the-loop escalation."}], "critical_failures": ["Describes a single monolithic LLM prompt without agent decomposition or state routing.", "Fails to specify how subagents coordinate or pass execution state."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-226"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Models & Context Engineering', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How do you implement feedback loops, tool restrictions, and memory limits in agent-based systems?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Models & Context Engineering", "subcategory": "Memory & Context Engineering", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "MEMORY_AND_STATE", "reference_answer": "Production agent memory separates short-term working context (active scratchpad within the model''s context window) from long-term persistent storage. Short-term context is optimized using sliding windows, token budgets, and recursive summarization to prevent attention dilution and token exhaustion. Long-term memory persists episodic conversation history and extracted entities (e.g., claim/ticket IDs) in Redis or Vector/Relational databases, retrieving them dynamically via similarity or structured metadata queries.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Short-Term vs Long-Term Memory Tiering: Differentiates short-term working context from persistent long-term storage", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Short-term context window buffer vs external database persistence", "Redis for fast session cache", "Vector or relational databases for persistent entity memory"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Entity Extraction & Context Injection: Explains entity extraction, indexing, and dynamic context injection techniques", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Structured entity extraction (e.g., claim numbers, user IDs)", "Dynamic injection into system prompts or tool parameters", "Context relevance filtering"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Context Window Optimization & Privacy: Covers context window management, compaction, and privacy/retention policies", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Sliding window buffering and summarization", "PII sanitization before persisting to long-term storage", "TTL and cache invalidation policies"]}], "critical_failures": ["Claims the entire raw chat history should be injected into every prompt indefinitely", "Cannot distinguish between in-memory session state and persistent storage"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-227"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How do you integrate preprocessing pipelines with downstream components such as agent memory and tool-calling mechanisms?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Models & Context Engineering", "subcategory": "Memory & Context Engineering", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "MEMORY_AND_STATE", "reference_answer": "Production agent memory separates short-term working context (active scratchpad within the model''s context window) from long-term persistent storage. Short-term context is optimized using sliding windows, token budgets, and recursive summarization to prevent attention dilution and token exhaustion. Long-term memory persists episodic conversation history and extracted entities (e.g., claim/ticket IDs) in Redis or Vector/Relational databases, retrieving them dynamically via similarity or structured metadata queries.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Short-Term vs Long-Term Memory Tiering: Differentiates short-term working context from persistent long-term storage", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Short-term context window buffer vs external database persistence", "Redis for fast session cache", "Vector or relational databases for persistent entity memory"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Entity Extraction & Context Injection: Explains entity extraction, indexing, and dynamic context injection techniques", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Structured entity extraction (e.g., claim numbers, user IDs)", "Dynamic injection into system prompts or tool parameters", "Context relevance filtering"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Context Window Optimization & Privacy: Covers context window management, compaction, and privacy/retention policies", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Sliding window buffering and summarization", "PII sanitization before persisting to long-term storage", "TTL and cache invalidation policies"]}], "critical_failures": ["Claims the entire raw chat history should be injected into every prompt indefinitely", "Cannot distinguish between in-memory session state and persistent storage"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-228"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How do you measure and evaluate the quality of outputs in an agentic AI system at both node and system levels?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "AI Systems Ops: Evaluation, Guardrails & Observability", "subcategory": "Evaluation & Observability", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "Evaluating agentic AI requires multi-tier metrics and distributed observability. Component-level metrics evaluate intent routing accuracy, tool selection precision/recall, and parameter schema validity. Workflow-level metrics track end-to-end task success rate, execution trajectory efficiency (step count), latency, and cost per task. Observability is achieved through distributed tracing (e.g., OpenTelemetry, LangSmith) capturing hierarchical spans across agent reasoning hops, tool execution latency, and model token usage for debugging silent failures.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Agent Roles & Coordination", "weight": 40, "description": "Explains agent roles, supervisor/orchestrator responsibilities, and routing mechanisms."}, {"name": "State & Communication", "weight": 30, "description": "Details shared state management, tool usage, and inter-agent message passing."}, {"name": "Validation & Fallback", "weight": 30, "description": "Covers tool validation, retry mechanisms, and human-in-the-loop escalation."}], "critical_failures": ["Describes a single monolithic LLM prompt without agent decomposition or state routing.", "Fails to specify how subagents coordinate or pass execution state."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-229"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How do you translate a business workflow or problem statement into an agentic AI system design?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Multi-Agent System Architecture", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "The system employs a hierarchical supervisor-worker multi-agent architecture to manage complex domain tasks. An Orchestrator/Supervisor agent maintains global workflow state, performs intent classification, and delegates domain sub-tasks to specialized domain agents (e.g., Knowledge Agent, Transaction Agent, Policy Agent). Agents communicate via structured schemas over a shared state graph. Tools are decoupled from reasoning, exposed as typed functions with strict parameter validation. Resilience is ensured through self-reflection loops for transient errors, recursion depth limits to prevent infinite execution cycles, and escalation paths to human operators.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Multi-Agent Roles & Architecture: Defines clear agent roles, responsibilities, and separation of concerns across domain boundaries", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Supervisor / Orchestrator coordination pattern", "Specialized domain workers with dedicated tools", "Decoupling reasoning from execution"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Coordination, State & Communication: Explains how state and message payloads flow between supervisor and domain agents", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Shared state dictionary and context passing", "Structured input/output contracts between agents", "Parallel vs sequential sub-task execution"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Validation, Fallback & Escalation: Covers error handling, infinite loop prevention, guardrail validation, and human handoff", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Step/depth limits (max iterations)", "Output validation and reflection loops", "Graceful fallback and human escalation"]}], "critical_failures": ["Designs a monolithic prompt instead of specialized agent roles", "Lacks mechanisms to prevent infinite agent execution loops"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-230"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How does LangGraph improve upon LangChain for agentic workflows, and how did you apply it in your system?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "LangGraph & Stateful Orchestration", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "LANGGRAPH_ORCHESTRATION", "reference_answer": "LangGraph models agent workflows as cyclic computational graphs using StateGraph, nodes, and conditional edges. Nodes represent compute steps or agent reasoning tasks, while conditional routing functions determine dynamic transitions based on state. State is persisted across cycles and multi-turn conversations using thread-level checkpointers (e.g. Postgres, SQLite, Redis), enabling resumable workflows, human-in-the-loop interrupts, and rollback capabilities.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Graph Architecture & Control Flow: Explains StateGraph, nodes, edges, conditional routing, and cyclic execution", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Nodes representing agents or tools", "Conditional edges for dynamic runtime routing", "Cyclic execution loops vs linear DAGs"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "State Schema & Reducers: Details typed state schema, message passing, and state reducer functions", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Typed state dictionary (TypedDict / Pydantic)", "State reducers (e.g., add_messages) for combining updates", "Maintaining conversation history and tool results in graph state"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Persistence & Human-in-the-Loop: Covers checkpointing, human-in-the-loop interrupts, and failure recovery", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Checkpointer persistence (Postgres, SQLite, Redis)", "Interrupting before/after nodes for human approval", "State rollback, branching, and time-travel debugging"]}], "critical_failures": ["Asserts LangGraph is strictly a linear pipeline identical to basic LangChain chains", "Cannot explain how state is updated or passed between graph nodes"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-231"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'How does it actually work when you say that you have implemented MCP so that you can use legacy APIs? How exactly MCP servers can be developed for these legacy [APIs]?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Model Context Protocol & Tools", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "TOOL_INTEGRATION_MCP", "reference_answer": "Model Context Protocol (MCP) standardizes how models discover, authorize, and invoke external resources and tools via JSON-RPC 2.0 (over stdio or SSE). It decouples client agent reasoning from server-side tool execution, providing standardized schemas, access control, and isolation. Trade-offs in production include RPC latency, authorization overhead, and debugging complexity compared to native direct function calls.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "MCP Architecture & Transport: Explains client-server decoupling, JSON-RPC 2.0, and dynamic tool discovery", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Decouples model host/client from tool server", "Uses JSON-RPC 2.0 via stdio or SSE/HTTP", "Dynamic schema discovery without hardcoding"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Security, Authorization & Schemas: Details typed JSON schemas, role-based authorization, and isolated tool execution", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Typed JSON schema validation for parameters", "Role-based authorization and tool permissions (RBAC)", "Execution isolation and sandboxing"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Production Trade-offs & Adoption: Evaluates latency overhead, enterprise readiness, and comparison with direct function calls", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["RPC overhead and network latency", "Standardization across multiple agents vs custom code", "Observability, distributed tracing, and error handling"]}], "critical_failures": ["Confuses MCP with a proprietary model architecture rather than a protocol", "Claims MCP eliminates the need for parameter schema validation"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-232"}', 1),
