('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'Talk me through why you chose the LangGraph ecosystem versus others.', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "LangGraph & Stateful Orchestration", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "LANGGRAPH_ORCHESTRATION", "reference_answer": "LangGraph models agent workflows as cyclic computational graphs using StateGraph, nodes, and conditional edges. Nodes represent compute steps or agent reasoning tasks, while conditional routing functions determine dynamic transitions based on state. State is persisted across cycles and multi-turn conversations using thread-level checkpointers (e.g. Postgres, SQLite, Redis), enabling resumable workflows, human-in-the-loop interrupts, and rollback capabilities.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Graph Architecture & Control Flow: Explains StateGraph, nodes, edges, conditional routing, and cyclic execution", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Nodes representing agents or tools", "Conditional edges for dynamic runtime routing", "Cyclic execution loops vs linear DAGs"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "State Schema & Reducers: Details typed state schema, message passing, and state reducer functions", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Typed state dictionary (TypedDict / Pydantic)", "State reducers (e.g., add_messages) for combining updates", "Maintaining conversation history and tool results in graph state"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Persistence & Human-in-the-Loop: Covers checkpointing, human-in-the-loop interrupts, and failure recovery", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Checkpointer persistence (Postgres, SQLite, Redis)", "Interrupting before/after nodes for human approval", "State rollback, branching, and time-travel debugging"]}], "critical_failures": ["Asserts LangGraph is strictly a linear pipeline identical to basic LangChain chains", "Cannot explain how state is updated or passed between graph nodes"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-270"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Generative AI & LLMs', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'Think about a second use case. There is a bug in the Jira. You have to read that bug. You have the code base from GitHub, and you need to do an RCA and give a recommended code fix and a recommended test. Tell me in the same manner what will be your agents, which LLM you prefer, how much will be your temperature and Top-P, and what are the different tools?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Multi-Agent System Architecture", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "The system employs a hierarchical supervisor-worker multi-agent architecture to manage complex domain tasks. An Orchestrator/Supervisor agent maintains global workflow state, performs intent classification, and delegates domain sub-tasks to specialized domain agents (e.g., Knowledge Agent, Transaction Agent, Policy Agent). Agents communicate via structured schemas over a shared state graph. Tools are decoupled from reasoning, exposed as typed functions with strict parameter validation. Resilience is ensured through self-reflection loops for transient errors, recursion depth limits to prevent infinite execution cycles, and escalation paths to human operators.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Multi-Agent Roles & Architecture: Defines clear agent roles, responsibilities, and separation of concerns across domain boundaries", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Supervisor / Orchestrator coordination pattern", "Specialized domain workers with dedicated tools", "Decoupling reasoning from execution"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Coordination, State & Communication: Explains how state and message payloads flow between supervisor and domain agents", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Shared state dictionary and context passing", "Structured input/output contracts between agents", "Parallel vs sequential sub-task execution"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Validation, Fallback & Escalation: Covers error handling, infinite loop prevention, guardrail validation, and human handoff", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Step/depth limits (max iterations)", "Output validation and reflection loops", "Graceful fallback and human escalation"]}], "critical_failures": ["Designs a monolithic prompt instead of specialized agent roles", "Lacks mechanisms to prevent infinite agent execution loops"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-271"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Models & Context Engineering', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What approach did you take to incorporate memory into your agent-based system, and how did it improve performance or user experience?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Models & Context Engineering", "subcategory": "Memory & Context Engineering", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "MEMORY_AND_STATE", "reference_answer": "Production agent memory separates short-term working context (active scratchpad within the model''s context window) from long-term persistent storage. Short-term context is optimized using sliding windows, token budgets, and recursive summarization to prevent attention dilution and token exhaustion. Long-term memory persists episodic conversation history and extracted entities (e.g., claim/ticket IDs) in Redis or Vector/Relational databases, retrieving them dynamically via similarity or structured metadata queries.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Short-Term vs Long-Term Memory Tiering: Differentiates short-term working context from persistent long-term storage", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Short-term context window buffer vs external database persistence", "Redis for fast session cache", "Vector or relational databases for persistent entity memory"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Entity Extraction & Context Injection: Explains entity extraction, indexing, and dynamic context injection techniques", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Structured entity extraction (e.g., claim numbers, user IDs)", "Dynamic injection into system prompts or tool parameters", "Context relevance filtering"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Context Window Optimization & Privacy: Covers context window management, compaction, and privacy/retention policies", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Sliding window buffering and summarization", "PII sanitization before persisting to long-term storage", "TTL and cache invalidation policies"]}], "critical_failures": ["Claims the entire raw chat history should be injected into every prompt indefinitely", "Cannot distinguish between in-memory session state and persistent storage"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-272"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What approach would you use to generate matches''agentic AI, traditional machine learning, or a hybrid''and what are the trade-offs?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Agentic AI & Multi-Agent Systems", "subcategory": "Architectural Trade-offs & Comparisons", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "ARCHITECTURE_EXPLANATION", "reference_answer": "Agentic architectures introduce autonomy, dynamic tool execution, and flexible problem-solving, but at the cost of non-determinism, multi-second latency, token expenditure, and debugging complexity. Deterministic software or static linear pipelines are preferred for exact arithmetic, ACID compliance, low-latency APIs, and fixed sequential business logic. When scaling from single-agent to multi-agent, single agents suffer from prompt bloat and tool confusion, while multi-agent systems provide clear separation of concerns at the cost of orchestration latency.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Agent Roles & Coordination", "weight": 40, "description": "Explains agent roles, supervisor/orchestrator responsibilities, and routing mechanisms."}, {"name": "State & Communication", "weight": 30, "description": "Details shared state management, tool usage, and inter-agent message passing."}, {"name": "Validation & Fallback", "weight": 30, "description": "Covers tool validation, retry mechanisms, and human-in-the-loop escalation."}], "critical_failures": ["Describes a single monolithic LLM prompt without agent decomposition or state routing.", "Fails to specify how subagents coordinate or pass execution state."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-273"}', 1),
