red": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Supervisor / Orchestrator coordination pattern", "Specialized domain workers with dedicated tools", "Decoupling reasoning from execution"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Coordination, State & Communication: Explains how state and message payloads flow between supervisor and domain agents", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Shared state dictionary and context passing", "Structured input/output contracts between agents", "Parallel vs sequential sub-task execution"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Validation, Fallback & Escalation: Covers error handling, infinite loop prevention, guardrail validation, and human handoff", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Step/depth limits (max iterations)", "Output validation and reflection loops", "Graceful fallback and human escalation"]}], "critical_failures": ["Designs a monolithic prompt instead of specialized agent roles", "Lacks mechanisms to prevent infinite agent execution loops"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-318"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'AI Engineering', 'AI Systems Ops: Evaluation, Guardrails & Observability', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What strategies do you use to handle agent failures or dead ends, including fallback mechanisms, retries, observability, and debugging?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "AI Systems Ops: Evaluation, Guardrails & Observability", "subcategory": "Evaluation & Observability", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "EVALUATION_OBSERVABILITY", "reference_answer": "Evaluating agentic AI requires multi-tier metrics and distributed observability. Component-level metrics evaluate intent routing accuracy, tool selection precision/recall, and parameter schema validity. Workflow-level metrics track end-to-end task success rate, execution trajectory efficiency (step count), latency, and cost per task. Observability is achieved through distributed tracing (e.g., OpenTelemetry, LangSmith) capturing hierarchical spans across agent reasoning hops, tool execution latency, and model token usage for debugging silent failures.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"id": "C1", "description": "Evaluation Metrics & Benchmarks: Identifies component-level and workflow-level evaluation metrics", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["End-to-end task completion rate", "Tool selection accuracy and parameter validity", "Trajectory step efficiency, latency, and cost tracking"]}, {"id": "C2", "description": "Distributed Tracing & Observability: Explains distributed tracing infrastructure across agent reasoning hops and tools", "weight": 35, "required": true, "acceptable_evidence": ["Hierarchical spans (Agent -> Node -> Tool)", "OpenTelemetry / LangSmith tracing integration", "Capturing inputs, intermediate thoughts, and tool return payloads"]}, {"id": "C3", "description": "Feedback Loops & Continuous Improvement: Details golden evaluation datasets, LLM-as-a-judge, and feedback loop integration", "weight": 30, "required": false, "acceptable_evidence": ["Curated golden evaluation datasets representing edge cases", "Calibrated LLM-as-a-judge for semantic verification", "Continuous prompt and workflow iteration based on production traces"]}], "critical_failures": ["Relies solely on perplexity or BLEU scores to evaluate multi-step agent systems", "Lacks understanding of distributed tracing or step-by-step trajectory debugging"]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-319"}', 1),
('TECHNICAL', 'DevOps and Cloud', 'Containers & Orchestration', 'MEDIUM', 'SPECIFIC', 90, 'What tools and frameworks did you use for building and deploying your ML pipelines, including LangChain, Docker, Kubernetes, and CI/CD systems?', '{"version": 1, "rubric_version": "142.2", "topic": "Cloud Architecture & Services", "subcategory": "Cloud & Deployment Architecture", "question_type": "TECHNICAL_CONCEPT", "question_intent": "CLOUD_INFRASTRUCTURE", "reference_answer": "Deploying AI agents on AWS pairs containerized microservices with managed model endpoints. Agent orchestrators run in Docker containers on Amazon EKS (or ECS Fargate) utilizing Horizontal Pod Autoscaling (HPA) and stateless worker designs. Models are accessed via Amazon Bedrock with PrivateLink VPC endpoints for compliance and IAM least-privilege access. State is persisted in ElastiCache Redis (session state) and RDS/Aurora (checkpointing), with CloudWatch and AWS X-Ray providing distributed tracing and metric alarms.", "evidence_policy": {"acceptable_evidence_is_non_exhaustive": true, "semantic_equivalence_allowed": true, "evaluator_guidance": "acceptable_evidence lists representative examples of valid technical evidence, not mandatory keywords. Any technically equivalent demonstration or alternative valid architecture must receive full credit.", "criteria_weight_policy": "All criteria weights sum to 100 points. Criteria marked required: true represent non-negotiable core competency dimensions that require demonstrative evidence to pass the question. Criteria marked required: false represent architectural depth or operational nuance that additively contribute to the total score."}, "evaluation": {"criteria": [{"name": "Container & Pod Architecture", "weight": 40, "description": "Explains Docker/Kubernetes container orchestration, pod lifecycles, and deployment manifests."}, {"name": "Scaling & Resource Management", "weight": 30, "description": "Details autoscaling (HPA), resource limits, ingress routing, and service discovery."}, {"name": "Operational Reliability & Monitoring", "weight": 30, "description": "Covers health probes, zero-downtime rollouts, and container logging/observability."}], "critical_failures": ["Confuses basic container concepts or fails to explain container orchestration mechanics.", "Lacks understanding of Kubernetes resource allocation, scaling, or health checks."]}, "question_code": "TECH-AGT-320"}', 1),
